%% SECTION Governance
@@
The governance framework states that the project manager may approve changes up to $10,000; above that, the change control board decides. A change estimated at $25,000 is requested. What should the project manager do?
A) Analyse the impact and submit the request, with the analysis, to the change control board
B) Approve it, since the project manager is accountable for delivering the project outcomes
C) Split it into several smaller changes that each fall within the project manager's approval limit
D) Reject it, since changes above the project manager's threshold cannot be accommodated
= A
? Authority thresholds define who decides. The PM prepares the impact analysis and submits it to the CCB. Splitting the change to avoid governance is unethical.

@@
A change request was approved by the change control board. What should happen next?
A) Update the affected plans, baselines and change log, communicate it and implement it
B) Implement the change right away and update the documentation at the next phase gate
C) Ask the sponsor to confirm the decision before informing the team about the change
D) Hold the change until the next release so that the current baseline is not disturbed
= A
? Approved changes are reflected in plans and baselines, logged, communicated and then implemented in Manage Project Execution.

@@
A team member discovers that the version of a design drawing used on site is not the latest approved version. Which governance mechanism should have prevented this?
A) Configuration management
B) Stakeholder engagement planning
C) Resource levelling
D) Earned value management
= A
? Configuration management controls versions of product specifications and documents through identification, status accounting and verification.

@@
Which statement about the sponsor's role in governance is most accurate?
A) Champions the project, provides resources, decides beyond the PM's authority and owns benefits
B) Manages the team's daily work and assigns tasks so that the project stays on schedule and budget
C) Develops the detailed schedule and cost estimates on behalf of the project manager and team
D) Approves every work package and activity before the team is allowed to start working on it
= A
? Sponsors provide direction, funding and support, and resolve escalated issues; they do not manage day-to-day work.

@@
A project manager wants to capture tacit knowledge from senior engineers before they retire during the project. Which approach is most effective?
A) Pair junior engineers with the senior engineers and run job-shadowing and storytelling sessions
B) Ask the senior engineers to complete a detailed survey about all of their experience before leaving
C) Copy the senior engineers' email archives and documents to the shared project repository
D) Invite the senior engineers to the final lessons learned meeting at the end of the project
= A
? Tacit knowledge transfers best through interaction such as pairing, shadowing and storytelling, and should be captured while people are still available.

@@
During a quality audit, the auditor finds that the team is not following the agreed peer-review procedure. Which process does this audit belong to?
A) Manage Quality Assurance
B) Monitor and Control Scope
C) Validate Scope
D) Close Project or Phase
= A
? Audits of process compliance are part of quality assurance in the Governance domain.

@@
The project's phase gate review is scheduled, but the team is confident the project is on track. What is the main purpose of still holding the gate review?
A) To confirm the project still aligns with the business case and authorise the next phase
B) To approve the team's planned leave and confirm resource calendars for the next phase
C) To re-estimate every remaining activity so that the whole schedule baseline can be refreshed
D) To close the procurement contracts that supported the work of the completed phase
= A
? Phase gates are governance decision points that confirm continued justification and authorise continuation, even when performance is good.

@@
What is the purpose of the change log?
A) To track change requests, their status and the decisions made
B) To record identified risks together with their planned responses
C) To capture knowledge gained so that future projects can benefit
D) To record how each stakeholder has been classified and prioritised
= A
? The change log tracks change requests submitted, their impact assessments, decisions and status.

@@
A software vendor's project manager agrees verbally with the buyer's project manager to add three reports at no cost. What is the main problem?
A) Contract changes must go through contract change control and be authorised in writing
B) Verbal agreements between project managers are illegal in most jurisdictions
C) Reports cannot be part of a contract, so they must be procured separately
D) There is no problem, because the seller has agreed to add the reports to the contract at no cost
= A
? Contract changes require formal documentation and approval by authorised parties. Informal changes create disputes and uncontrolled scope.

@@
Which contract type is most suitable for a five-year supply agreement in which the cost of steel may fluctuate significantly?
A) Fixed price with economic price adjustment
B) Firm fixed price
C) Cost plus award fee
D) Time and materials
= A
? FP-EPA contracts include adjustments for defined economic changes, such as commodity prices, over long periods.

@@
A buyer cannot define the scope of a research project. Which contract type places most cost risk on the buyer while still motivating the seller through a fee tied to performance?
A) Cost plus incentive fee
B) Firm fixed price
C) Fixed price incentive fee
D) Fixed price with economic price adjustment
= A
? Cost-reimbursable contracts suit uncertain scope; CPIF links the fee to performance against targets.

@@
Which document describes the items to be procured in sufficient detail for prospective sellers to determine whether they can provide them?
A) Procurement statement of work
B) Source selection criteria
C) Bid evaluation report
D) Seller's proposal
= A
? The procurement SOW describes the item or service to be procured; source selection criteria explain how proposals will be evaluated.

@@
A project is cancelled after 40% of the work is done. Which activity is still required?
A) Documenting the reasons and status, closing contracts and capturing lessons learned
B) Nothing, because closure activities apply only to projects that finish successfully
C) Completing the remaining work so that all deliverables are available for the record
D) Archiving the charter only, since other documents are no longer relevant to anyone
= A
? Projects closed early still need closure activities to settle obligations, transfer partial work and preserve knowledge.

@@ | multi
Which two practices reflect governance tailored for adaptive delivery? (Choose two.)
A) Funding the product team incrementally based on demonstrated value
B) Using guardrails such as budget limits and architecture standards within which the team decides
C) Requiring CCB approval for every user story change
D) Freezing all requirements before the first sprint
E) Holding weekly 4-hour status meetings with all developers
= A, B
? Lightweight governance uses incremental funding and guardrails. Detailed CCB control of stories and frozen requirements contradict adaptive delivery.

@@
A project manager observes that the team keeps working on change requests that were never approved because "the customer asked nicely". What should the project manager do?
A) Reinforce the change control process with the team and customer, and assess the work done
B) Accept the practice, because customer satisfaction is the most important success criterion
C) Remove the customer's access to the team for the rest of the project to stop new requests
D) Stop the project until the customer agrees in writing never to request changes again
= A
? Unapproved changes cause scope creep. The PM coaches the team and the customer on the agreed process and assesses the impact of work already done.

@@
Which output of Manage Project Execution provides the raw data used by the monitoring and controlling processes?
A) Work performance data
B) Work performance reports
C) Accepted deliverables
D) The project charter
= A
? Executing processes produce work performance data, which control processes analyse into information and then reports.

@@
A project manager receives a change request from the client. After impact analysis, the change would delay the project by three weeks and add $40,000. The client insists the change is essential. What is the best next step?
A) Present the impact analysis and options to the change control board or authorised decision-maker
B) Implement the change, since the client considers it essential and will accept the resulting delay later
C) Refuse the change, since it increases cost and the cost baseline has already been approved
D) Ask the team to absorb the work through overtime so that the baseline does not need to change
= A
? The decision belongs to those with authority, who need the analysis and options. The PM neither refuses nor implements unilaterally.

@@
Which artifact gives the project manager the authority to apply organisational resources?
A) Project charter
B) Project management plan
C) Business case
D) Team charter
= A
? The charter formally authorises the project and the PM's authority.

@@
During closure, the project manager realises that some lessons were never captured. Which practice would have prevented this?
A) Recording lessons learned throughout the project in the lessons learned register
B) Holding a longer lessons learned workshop at the end of the project with all members
C) Asking the sponsor to write the lessons learned, since the sponsor has the full overview
D) Capturing lessons learned only on projects that encountered serious problems
= A
? Lessons should be recorded continuously, when memories are fresh and while they can still improve the current project.

@@
Which source selection method evaluates proposals on a combination of technical quality and price using weighted criteria?
A) Quality and cost-based selection
B) Least cost
C) Sole source
D) Qualifications only
= A
? Quality and cost-based selection weighs both technical merit and price. Least cost suits standard items; sole source has one supplier.

@@
What is the main difference between corrective and preventive actions?
A) Corrective actions realign current performance; preventive actions keep future performance aligned
B) Corrective actions repair defective products; preventive actions update the project documents
C) Corrective actions are approved by the sponsor; preventive actions are approved by the team
D) Corrective actions are used only in predictive projects, and preventive actions only in agile projects
= A
? Corrective action addresses deviations that have occurred; preventive action reduces the chance of future deviations.

@@
A project in a regulated industry needs a decision about a design change that affects safety. The project manager's authority covers only cost changes under $5,000. The change costs $2,000. What should the project manager consider?
A) Whether governance requires safety-related changes to be approved by a specific authority
B) Approving it at once, since the cost is well below the project manager's own approval threshold
C) Implementing it immediately, since safety changes are urgent and bypass governance
D) Asking the team to vote on the change, since they understand the design best
= A
? Governance can define thresholds by impact type (safety, compliance), not only cost. The PM checks the decision rights before acting.

@@
Which meeting marks the end of planning and the start of execution in a predictive project?
A) Kickoff meeting
B) Sprint retrospective
C) Bidder conference
D) Close-out meeting
= A
? The kickoff meeting launches execution by aligning stakeholders on objectives, roles and the plan.

@@
The steering committee asks for a single integrated view of progress for a hybrid project with predictive hardware work and agile software work. What should the project manager provide?
A) An integrated report combining milestones, release burnup, key risks and forecasts
B) Two separate reports, one for hardware and one for software, without a combined view
C) Only the hardware Gantt chart, since the software scope is flexible and changes often
D) A list of individual developers' velocity so the committee can compare productivity
= A
? Hybrid governance benefits from one coherent story combining predictive and adaptive measures, without exposing inappropriate individual metrics.

@@
What is the PMO's typical role in a controlling PMO model?
A) Provides support and requires compliance with frameworks, templates and reviews
B) Directly manages all of the projects, with every project manager reporting to the PMO
C) Offers optional templates and training without any compliance requirements
D) Approves the sponsor's budget and benefits targets for each project
= A
? Controlling PMOs provide support and require compliance; directive PMOs manage projects; supportive PMOs consult.
